DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The vvsslloocckk() and vvssuunnlloocckk() functions respectively lock and unlock a
     range of addresses belonging to the currently running process into mem‐
     ory.  The actual amount of memory locked is a multiple of the machine's
     page size.  The starting page number is computed by truncating _a_d_d_r to
     the nearest preceding page boundary, and by rounding up _a_d_d_r _+ _l_e_n to the
     next page boundary.  The process context to use for this operation is
     taken from the global variable _c_u_r_p_r_o_c.

RREETTUURRNN VVAALLUUEESS
     The vvsslloocckk() function will return 0 on success, otherwise it will return
     one of the errors listed below.

EERRRROORRSS
     The vvsslloocckk() function will fail if:

     [EINVAL]           The _a_d_d_r and _l_e_n parameters specify a memory range
                        that wraps around the end of the machine address
                        space.

     [ENOMEM]           The size of the specified address range exceeds the
                        system limit on locked memory.

     [EFAULT]           Some portion of the indicated address range is not al‐
                        located.  There was an error faulting/mapping a page.

